## Context

Child of ticket 1507 (tracker). The author split the work on 2026-11-10: the
Codex part first (ticket 1508), the Pi part by a second raid. Ticket 1508
builds the per-runtime seam in `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py` (a runtime is
a corpus root plus a reader) and the per-runtime reporting; this ticket adds
the Pi reader on that seam. It executes on main after ticket 1508 has merged:
both edit the same script.

Observed on padme, 2026-11-10 (key names only, no transcript content read):
78 `*.jsonl` files under `~/.pi/agent/sessions`, the newest dated 2026-11-01.
Assistant records carry `message.usage` with `input`, `output`, `cacheRead`,
`cacheWrite` and `totalTokens`; a `model_change` record names the model.

Not established: whether `input` already includes `cacheRead` and
`cacheWrite`. Settle it on one transcript before summing.

## Scope

- A reader for Pi sessions on the seam ticket 1508 lands, same definition of
  context length as ticket 1499: the whole prompt of a turn.
- The audit's table and JSON count Pi sessions under their runtime; Pi leaves
  the not-read list.
- An absent or unreadable Pi corpus is reported as could not look, never as
  zero sessions.

Out of scope: a status line or live display on Pi; compaction and missed-run
cost on Pi; ceilings for local models served to Pi (they take the default).

## Test

On a Pi fixture transcript: a session past its model's ceiling is counted
under Pi; a turn outside the window is not counted; an absent Pi corpus is
reported as could not look while the other runtimes are still counted.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The audit counts Pi main sessions beside Claude Code's and Codex's,
  with the whole-prompt definition, and its output names Pi as read
- [ ] An unreadable or absent Pi corpus is reported as could not look, the
  other runtimes still counted
- [ ] Whether Pi's `input` includes its cache fields is answered in the
  reader's docstring, with the transcript evidence
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Imagine (raid 626455)

Why now: unchanged since the author opened 1507 on 2026-11-10. Premise
checked on padme the same day, key names and numbers only, on all 78
`*.jsonl` files under `~/.pi/agent/sessions` (mtime 2026-09-16 to
2026-11-01):

- Layout: `<encoded cwd>/<iso time>_<uuid>.jsonl`, 14 directories, depth 2.
  The first line is a `session` header with `id` (the uuid of the file name),
  `timestamp`, `cwd` and `version` (3 on all 78). No header carries
  `parentSession`, which Pi's `docs/session-format.md` says `/fork` and
  `/clone` set.
- Records: `message` 1699, `model_change` 79, `thinking_level_change` 78,
  `context_edit` 30, `custom_message` 17, `compaction` 7. Every entry has
  `id` and `parentId` (a tree); no entry has two children in this corpus.
- 763 assistant messages carry `message.model`, `message.provider` and
  `message.usage` with `input`, `output`, `cacheRead`, `cacheWrite`,
  `totalTokens` (`cacheWrite1h` on 13, zero on all 13). `responseId` is on
  715, unique within its file.
- The cache question: `totalTokens == input + output + cacheRead +
  cacheWrite` on 763 of 763, and `cacheRead > input` on 632. So `input`
  excludes both cache fields, and the whole prompt is `input + cacheRead +
  cacheWrite`. Pi's own `dist/core/usage-totals.js` treats `cacheWrite1h` as
  a split of `cacheWrite`, not an addend.
- 64 assistant messages carry `errorMessage` and an all-zero usage: not
  turns. The 48 without a `responseId` are all among them.
- Record `timestamp` is ISO 8601 with milliseconds and `Z` on all 1988
  stamped records, which `ts._parse_ts` already reads.
- Subagents: Pi's subagent extension spawns children with `--no-session`
  (`~/.pi/agent/extensions/subagent/index.ts`), so no Pi subagent transcript
  exists under this root. Every file here is a main session, and the header
  has no field that tells an interactive session from a `pi -p` run. (The
  Blind-Spot pass found the seat spool, below.)
- Largest whole prompt in any file: 114638 tokens; none reaches 230K. Turns
  are mostly local models (`qwen3.8-27b` 529, `qwen3.8-flash-next-iq3_s`
  180), a few hosted ones (`claude-opus-5-5`, `gpt-5.5`, `gpt-6-sol`). The
  live Pi count past any ceiling will be zero: the positive control must
  come from fixtures, and "read, zero sessions past" must stay distinct from
  "could not look".
- Corpus root: Pi honours a `sessionDir` setting, the environment variable
  `PI_CODING_AGENT_SESSION_DIR` and `--session-dir`; padme sets none. The
  harness resolves the root through `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` (below).

Simplest path: one reader function on the 1508 seam, nothing else.
Session id from the header `id`, project from the header `cwd`, agent id
always `main`, model per turn from `message.model`, date from the record
`timestamp`, whole prompt as above, error turns skipped, `msg_id` set to
`pi:` plus `responseId` so a `/fork` or `/clone` copy is not counted twice by
`drop_inherited_turns`. Pi enters the session counts only, like Codex: no
compaction events, no missed-run dollars. An absent root is could not look; a
root that holds no file in the window is read, with zero sessions. Not used:
`model_change` (the model is on each turn), the tree (each turn's usage
already measures the prompt of the branch it was sent on), `context_edit`.

No library parses Pi sessions in Python; Pi's own reader is TypeScript. No
YAGNI or abstraction hit: the reader is one function and one table entry.

Later review: the tracker's integration review runs the audit on padme's
live Pi corpus and checks the Pi session count against the file count in
the window by hand (zero past ceiling expected, see above).

Blind-Spot pass (2026-11-10, one read-only seat): not clean, four findings,
each checked by the lead on key names and numbers:

1. A second Pi corpus: the archive spool `<archive>/spool/padme/pi`
   (ticket 1383; `scripts/transcript_spool.py` `spool_dir('pi')`) holds 28
   files dated 2026-10-31 to 2026-11-02, header ids disjoint from the 78
   live ones; the same usage identity holds there (634 of 634 turns), peak
   115032. The Pi seat template (`skills/route/routes.json`) runs
   `pi --session-dir <spool> -p`, and `scripts/pi-subagent-link` patches
   the subagent extension to write there; on padme today the extension
   symlink is the unpatched one, so "Pi subagents leave no transcript" above
   holds for this host now, not by design. The raw archive (106 files) is a
   copy of both and is not read.
2. No role rule: `pi -p` without `--session-dir` also writes to the live
   root, and no header field tells it from an interactive session.
3. The harness already resolves the live root with `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR`
   (`transcript_spool.live_roots()`), and `scripts/trace_events.py`
   `read_pi` already parses Pi sessions (model from `model_change`, then
   `message.model`). Per-turn `message.model` equals the latest
   `model_change.modelId` on 699 of 699 live and 634 of 634 spool turns.
4. The live check passes vacuously on the default window (newest file
   2026-11-02, none past 230K); it needs a positive control.

Decisions taken by default (reversible, inside the ticket's scope):

- The reader reads both roots from `transcript_spool.source_roots('pi')`.
  Live-root sessions are main sessions held to their model's ceiling, the
  rule Claude Code main sessions already follow (a main session is not
  known to be an interface session). Spool sessions are launched seats or
  workers: counted as Pi sessions held to `default_tokens`, the rule ticket
  1508 gives Codex `exec`. The output prints which rule each count used.
  Rejected: naming the spool not read (it would leave out the newest Pi
  files and every seat run); counting spool files as interface sessions
  (they are launched by construction).
- An unknown archive root (`archive_root()` raises) or an absent spool is
  could not look for the spool only; the live root is still counted.
- The model of a turn is `message.model` (agreement above); the reader
  reuses `read_pi` for turns unless its events lack something the audit
  needs, in which case the docstring says what.
- Positive control for the live run: `--since 2026-09-16`, Pi peak checked
  by hand against 115032 (spool) and 114638 (live), session count against
  78 + 28 files. Sent to the 1507 coordinator for its integration review.

## Plan (raid 626455)

Written by the raid lead against main at a7a5f202 and the seam as ticket
1508's plan describes it; to be re-checked against the merged seam before
Execute. Hunt's Plan phase owns the detail.

Base: main with ticket 1508 merged.

Actions:

1. First test, red for the targeted reason: a Pi fixture root with one
   session on `claude-opus-5-5` holding 30 or more in-window assistant turns
   whose `input` is small and whose `cacheRead` puts the whole prompt past
   the Opus ceiling, beside a Claude Code fixture root; the audit counts one
   Pi session past its ceiling under Pi and leaves the Claude Code counts as
   they are.
2. The reader: header, assistant turns (from `trace_events.read_pi` where
   it fits), error turns skipped, `msg_id` namespaced; the docstring answers
   the cache question with the evidence above.
3. The table entry: Pi's roots from `transcript_spool.source_roots('pi')`
   (live root via `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR`, then the spool), each with its own
   status and role rule, and a CLI override in the shape 1508 gives the
   Codex root. Pi leaves the not-read list.
4. Tests for the window cut, the role rule (the same turns past 230K and
   under the Opus ceiling: counted past ceiling from the spool, not from the
   live root) and could not look per root (below).

Tests that would pass on a wrong implementation, and the fix:

- A fixture with `cacheRead == 0` passes whether or not the reader adds
  the cache fields. The past-ceiling fixture puts most of the prompt in
  `cacheRead` and `cacheWrite`, so reading `input` alone stays under.
- A fixture whose turns all carry usage cannot catch counting error turns.
  Add zero-usage `errorMessage` turns to an otherwise short session, enough
  to cross `LONG_SESSION_TURNS` if they were counted; assert they are not.
- A window test whose session lies wholly outside passes if the reader
  dates turns from the header. Put the header inside the window and the
  turns outside (and the reverse) and assert on the turn count.
- A could-not-look test on the exit code alone passes if Pi is silently
  dropped. Assert Pi is named could not look, absent from the counts, and
  the other runtimes' counts equal a run without the Pi root.
- A `/clone` fixture (second file, `parentSession` header, same entries and
  `responseId`s plus new turns) checks only the new turns are counted.

Dependencies: ticket 1508 merged.

## Feasibility (raid 626455)

PASS on the current tree, WARN on the seam, read by the raid lead at
a7a5f202:

- `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py`: `parse_trajectory` events are
  `{"kind", "cache_read", "prompt", "model", "msg_id", "ts"}`;
  `drop_inherited_turns` dedups on `msg_id` corpus-wide and skips turns
  whose `msg_id` is None; `restrict_to_window` drops undated events;
  `ceiling_for` maps `claude-opus-5-5` to the Opus entry and every local
  Qwen id to `default_tokens` (230000).
- `ts._parse_ts` (`scripts/trace-stats.py`) reads `...Z` stamps.
- `tests/test_context_length_audit.py` builds fixtures with `_turn` and
  `_session`; the Pi fixture writes the header and message records the same
  way.
- No API key, no network, no transcript content read.
- WARN, seam: 1509 cannot be written until 1508 merges. The reader assumes
  the seam takes the session id and project from the reader (not from the
  path), lets a runtime have no subagents and no role field, accepts a
  per-turn `msg_id` for dedupe without requiring one, and keeps Pi out of the
  missed-run figures, and lets one runtime have two roots, each with its
  own status (read or could not look) and its own ceiling rule. Each went
  to the 1508 coordinator in this raid's first report, 2026-11-10.
- `scripts/transcript_spool.py`: `live_roots()`, `spool_dir(runtime)` and
  `source_roots(runtime)` exist; `archive_root()` raises `RuntimeError`
  when neither `IDH_ARCHIVE_ROOT` nor `DATASETS_DIR` resolves.
  `scripts/trace_events.py` `read_pi(lines, file, ...)` exists, returns
  `Event` tuples with epoch-second times and a `(file, entry id)` pointer.
- WARN, cross-ticket: PR 2397, ticket 1508 and this ticket edit the same
  script, in that order. No shared registry beyond the runtime table 1508
  creates, which this ticket extends by one entry.
